[[:db/retract 17592186381645 :answer-field/choices 17592186381648] [:db/retract 17592186381645 :answer-field/choices 17592186381649] [:db/retract 17592186381645 :answer-field/choices 17592186381650] [:db/retract 17592186381645 :answer-field/choices 17592186381646] [:db/retract 17592186381645 :answer-field/choices 17592186381647] {:db/id 17592186198259 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9d9ce0aa-3342-547e-8dda-214837653ef1"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198260 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-28a2abe2-d607-5ca5-a1d6-25d691cd727c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-9d9ce0aa-3342-547e-8dda-214837653ef1" :question/id #uuid "9d9ce0aa-3342-547e-8dda-214837653ef1" :question/math-academy-id "q-171836" :question/problem "![](images/10/10d3d9a3b026fc1511d9e7a57c1f3276606c9397f0045fdb7748e3038a42fb21.png)\n\nThe shaded regions above represent the truth sets of predicates $P(x)$ and $Q(x),$ defined over the universal set ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following is the truth set of $P(x)⇒Q(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $P(x)⇒Q(x)≡¬P(x)∨Q(x),$ the corresponding truth sets can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P⇒Q} & ={T}_{¬P∨Q} \\\\  & ={T}_{¬P}\\cup {T}_{Q} \\\\  & ={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cup {T}_{Q}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we sketch the set ${\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}:$ \n\n![](images/f0/f02e2138f843c5a350c8be6c81a408c4af51fb9b19ced3fa26bfc466493008ac.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, to get the truth set of $P(x)∨Q(x),$ we take all the points that belong to ${\\overset{―}{T}}_{p}$ or ${T}_{Q}:$\n\n![](images/45/45e38853893ceec19f969a1611cd50bbb9ff97e7bfd617c6e2e0a6268fb4b8fa.png)\n\nAs a result, we get the following diagram:\n\n![](images/dd/dde2aacce3d0707ddb09c06ca305645eadba1aedca9858eacc24abd46d09b03c.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-89c151cf-1aa4-5332-ba88-97da2a96de62"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-89c151cf-1aa4-5332-ba88-9